<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Misirlou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">known by the 1920s; earliest recording <a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Μισιρλού</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Misirlou&quot; is a folk song of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, from the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Its original author is not known, but Arabic, Greek rebetiko and Jewish klezmer musicians were all playing it by the 1920s. The title is the Greek feminine form of Misirlis, from the Turkish Mısırlı, &quot;Egyptian&quot;, so the song is about an Egyptian woman.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest known recording was made in 1927 for Columbia by the rebetiko musician Theodotos (Tetos) Demetriades, an Ottoman Greek born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> in 1897 who had moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1921, when most of the Greek speaking population fled the emerging Turkish state. He probably knew the tune as a folk song before he left. As with almost all early rebetika, the composer was never identified and ownership rested with the band leader. The claim that the tune derives from Bint Misr by the Egyptian musician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayed-darwish">Sayed Darwish</a> is thought to be incorrect.</p><p>The rebetiko version was meant for the Greek tsifteteli dance, at a slower tempo and in a different key from the Oriental performances known today. Michalis Patrinos recorded it in that style in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> around 1930 and again in New York City in 1931. The melody moves up and down the Hijaz Kar, or double harmonic, scale, and people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> claim it as a folk song of their own country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1945 Professor Brunhilde E. Dorsch organised an international dance group at Duquesne University in Pittsburgh to honour the allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>. The Cretan dance Syrtos Haniotikos had no music, and the student Pat Mandros Kazalas suggested the slower Misirlou tune. The new dance, first performed on 6 March 1945, took the song's name and spread in the Greek-American community, at Serbian Orthodox church dances as a kolo, and in the Armenian-American community, for which Reuben Sarkisian wrote Armenian words in Fresno in the early 1950s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1941 Nick Roubanis, a Greek-American music instructor, released a jazz instrumental arrangement crediting himself as composer; the claim was never challenged in law, so he is still credited everywhere except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, where credit goes to Roubanis or Patrinos. English lyrics followed, and the tune became an exotica standard. Harry James recorded it in 1941, Jan August in 1946 and Darío Moreno, in French, in 1951.</p><p>Dick Dale, whose Lebanese-American uncle had played the song on one string of the oud, turned it into a fast solo rock guitar piece in 1962. His surf version brought the song to a wider audience in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, was recorded by the Beach Boys in 1963 and opened the 1994 film Pulp Fiction. The Athens 2004 Olympics Organizing Committee selected it as one of the most influential Greek songs, and Anna Vissi sang it at the closing ceremony.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Misirlou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
